    thinking: "**Designing the Website Features**\n\nI\'m currently focused on the requirements for the \"Eventos\" section of the website. I have the main menu updates in progress, adding \"Eventos\" with smooth scrolling. I am also planning the public section, which will have the title \"Eventos\".\n\n\n**Analyzing Event Details**\n\nI\'ve been analyzing the event details to flesh out the \"Eventos\" section further. I\'m focusing on the descriptions and filters needed, like \"Pr\303\263ximos eventos,\" and sorting criteria. I\'m also planning how to format different image sizes, including both \"object-fit: contain\" and \"cover\" for optimal display. I\'m also thinking of using a background color with a blurred effect if spaces remain. Finally, I have ideas for the event card\'s design, including artwork, badges, and category labels.\n\n\n**Defining Event Interaction Features**\n\nMy focus has shifted to user interactions with events. I am working on the event card details. I am now planning the features for an event\'s page. The design will include artwork, event name, description, date, time, and address. There will also be a map, speakers, schedule, registration information, a share button, an \"add to calendar\" button, a gallery, and a back button. I\'m also planning the design of a featured event block for the homepage.\n\n\n**Expanding Event Data Details**\n\nNow, I\'m delving deeper into event data fields. I\'m focusing on map and registration links, deadlines, pricing, speaker details, schedules, and additional information for events. Also I\'m planning out image management for main art, stories, feeds, banners, galleries, with alt text, captions, and credits. I am also considering event settings like draft/publish status, and featured status.\n\n**Implementing Dummy Database**\n\nI\'ve transitioned to creating a mock database structure in code, mimicking a Supabase setup. I\'ll implement TypeScript types and a dummy database file (`src/lib/dummyEvents.ts`). This addresses the prompt\'s instruction to create equivalent tables for `events` and `event_media` as a starting point. This enables realistic app functionality without a live database. I\'m concentrating on the structure to prepare for integrating an actual DB.\n\n\n**Constructing Frontend Structure**\n\nI\'m now building out the frontend. I\'m focusing on UI representation using mock data to provide structural functionality, starting with menu updates and essential components like `EventsSection`, `FeaturedEvent`, `EventCard`, and `EventDetails`. I\'m also updating admin components for event management, and adding necessary routes in `App.tsx`. I am now structuring the `/eventos/:slug` route.\n\n\n**Refining Event Data Details**\n\nNow, I\'m fine-tuning the event data structure. I\'ve focused on date/time fields, handling optional end dates and times, and the `timezone` field. I\'m also implementing the `modality` with its options, including location and address details. I\'m also incorporating registration URLs, deadlines, and pricing information. I\'m structuring `speakers`, `schedule`, and `additional_information` fields, and the event\'s `status` and `is_featured` properties. Finally, I\'ve incorporated the database fields `display_order`, `created_at` and `updated_at`.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 526116976
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
