  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Presentation Aesthetics**\n\nI\'m currently focused on improving the visual appeal, specifically addressing the line breaks in the presentation. The user\'s feedback pointed me towards lines 29-33 within the JSX code, where I\'m now actively experimenting with alternative styling options to achieve a more pleasing aesthetic. I\'m aiming for a smoother flow and better visual hierarchy.\n\n\n**Analyzing Line Breaks**\n\nI\'ve determined the line breaks are due to container width constraints, not solely the `<br>` tag. The initial break after \"gera\" occurs because the container isn\'t wide enough for \"Seguran\303\247a jur\303\255dica que gera\". I\'m experimenting with removing the `<br>` or repositioning it to get a more desirable layout. I\'m also looking at setting a `max-width` on the heading to control the line breaks. I\'m aiming for \"Seguran\303\247a jur\303\255dica\" then \"que gera impacto positivo.\"\n\n\n**Optimizing Text Flow**\n\nI\'m now comparing two approaches to handle the text flow. Option A uses a `<br>` tag within the heading for a manual line break. Option B removes the `<br>` for natural wrapping, or uses `whitespace-nowrap` on a span element to prevent wrapping. I\'m focusing on how each affects readability and visual harmony, especially the handling of \"impacto positivo.\"\n\n\n**Adjusting Responsive Layout**\n\nI\'ve decided forcing \"impacto positivo\" to not wrap might cause problems on smaller screens. I\'m trying a `md:block` conditional break to make the split more intentional. I also considered a `max-w` to control natural line breaks or a `br` before \"que gera.\" The screenshot indicates the text size might be the culprit. I\'m focusing on the current implementation to see how it looks on different screen sizes.\n\n\n**Revising Code Structure**\n\nI\'ve pinpointed the root cause: the `<br>` tag is *after* \"gera,\" forcing the unwanted line breaks. \"Seguran\303\247a jur\303\255dica que\" takes up the first line, \"gera\" the second, and the `<br>` pushes \"impacto positivo\" to a third line. My immediate task is to rearrange the text and `<br>` to resolve this. I am using the suggested changes. I\'m focusing on \"lg:block\" to ensure proper breaks.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 10
      nanos: 593363555
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
